1. Everyday situations where simulation can be used
- A simulation can be useful at a supermarket checkout system.
It can be used to analyse how many checkout counters should be
open at different times of the day and how long customers have
to wait in queues.

- Simulation in particular is suitable because the number of
customers varies throughout the day. For example, supermarkets
are usually busier in the afternoon and during weekends.
It would be difficult to experiment with different
checkout arrangements in a real supermarket because it could
cause long queues and inconvenience customers. With simulation,
different situations can be tested safely without disturbing
the actual supermarket.

- Another example is public transportation. Simulation can analyse
bus schedules, passenger numbers and traffic conditions. It can help
determine how many buses are needed and how delays affect passengers.

- Simulation is also sustainable because we can test different
solutions before implementing them in real life.
That saves time and resources and can help organisations make more
sustainable decisions.

2. Analysis of a supermarket
- A supermarket is a suitable example of a system to analyse
using simulation because customer numbers, waiting times and available
resources constantly change. Simulation can help understand how
these elements affect each other and how the supermarket can improve its
operations.